---
import Learn from '../../layouts/Learn.astro';
import MemoryFlow from '../../components/learn/MemoryFlow.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/learn/BarChart.astro';
import { COMPANY, PRICES, TRIAL } from '../../data/site';

// Learn comparison page 1 (s94, founder: pages that name competitors so search
// and AI answers find us, "put facts not just wrong stories"). Rules as in
// data/pricing.ts (UK CAP Code 3.33 to 3.44, EU 2006/114/EC Art 4; UAE has no
// contrary rule, research s94): same need, their own words, no running down,
// names as plain text, dated. Every competitor fact was read on that company's
// own page on 2026-10-13 and is linked where it is made:
// - MemoryPlugin (memoryplugin.com, pricing on the home page): Core $89 a year,
//   Pro $180 a year, 7-day trial; "21+ AI tools"; "Encrypted in transit and at
//   rest"; where the data is kept is not stated.
// - Hjarni (hjarni.com): free up to 25 notes; Pro $12 a month, yearly billing
//   "two months free" ($120); ChatGPT and Claude directories plus MCP apps;
//   "EU-hosted (Hetzner)"; "Encrypted in transit & at rest".
// - Basic Memory (basicmemory.com, docs.basicmemory.com): Local $0, open source,
//   "Your files never leave your computer", plain Markdown; Cloud from $15 per
//   seat a month after a 7-day trial; no encryption statement.
// - Supermemory (supermemory.ai/pricing, /docs/supermemory-mcp/introduction):
//   Free $0 with $5 credits a month, Pro $19 a month; remote MCP server
//   mcp.supermemory.ai, OAuth; self-hosting on Scale and Enterprise. GitHub
//   (supermemoryai/supermemory): MIT; local version `npx supermemory local`,
//   data in ~/.supermemory.
// - Mem0 GitHub (mem0ai/mem0): Apache 2.0; "Self-Hosted Server" via Docker.
// - Mem0 (mem0.ai/pricing, docs.mem0.ai/platform/mem0-mcp): Hobby free, Starter
//   $19 a month; "Memories you store this way live in your Mem0 account, not on
//   your computer"; Node.js 18+ for the quick setup.
// The chart compares only apps for the same need (an everyday user's memory
// across AI apps), so Supermemory and Mem0, aimed at developers, are text only,
// as on the Pricing page.
const CHECKED = '13 October 2026';
const OUTLINE = [
  { id: 'what', title: 'What to compare' },
  { id: 'table', title: 'The apps side by side' },
  { id: 'apps', title: 'Each app in brief' },
  { id: 'price', title: 'What a year costs' },
  { id: 'limits', title: "Where Zaaheen falls short" },
  { id: 'choose', title: 'Which to choose' },
];
const MEMORYPLUGIN = 'https://www.memoryplugin.com/';
const HJARNI = 'https://hjarni.com/';
const BASIC = 'https://basicmemory.com/';
const BASIC_DOCS = 'https://docs.basicmemory.com/';
const SUPERMEMORY = 'https://supermemory.ai/pricing';
const SUPERMEMORY_MCP = 'https://supermemory.ai/docs/supermemory-mcp/introduction';
const SUPERMEMORY_GITHUB = 'https://github.com/supermemoryai/supermemory';
const MEM0 = 'https://mem0.ai/pricing';
const MEM0_MCP = 'https://docs.mem0.ai/platform/mem0-mcp';
---
<Learn
  path="/learn/ai-memory-apps-compared/"
  lede="Several apps now give ChatGPT, Claude and Cursor one memory they all share. The biggest difference between them is where your memories are kept: on the company's servers, or on your own computer. After that it comes down to which AI apps you use, how much setup you'll put up with, and price."
  outline={OUTLINE}
>
  <section id="what">
    <h2>What to compare</h2>
    <p>ChatGPT and Claude each have a memory of their own, and they don't share it. A memory
       app sits outside both and connects to each one, usually through MCP, the standard way AI
       apps plug into tools. Tell one app something and the others can find it later.</p>
    <p>We looked at five apps that do this, and we make one of them, so read this with that in
       mind. Every fact about the others comes from their own website, linked below, as we found
       it on {CHECKED}. Four questions sort them out quickly:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Where are your memories kept?</strong> On the company's servers, or on your
          computer.</li>
      <li><strong>Which AI apps does it reach?</strong> Some cover ChatGPT in a web browser and on
          your phone, others only apps on your computer.</li>
      <li><strong>How much setup?</strong> A normal installer, a sign-in, or commands typed into a
          terminal.</li>
      <li><strong>What does it cost after any free part ends?</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="table">
    <h2>The apps side by side</h2>
    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">App</th><th scope="col">Where memories are kept</th><th scope="col">Price</th><th scope="col">Made for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Zaaheen</th><td>Your computer, encrypted. Our servers never receive them</td><td>{TRIAL.days} days free, no card, then ${PRICES.monthly} a month or ${PRICES.yearly} a year</td><td>Everyday users of several AI apps</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">MemoryPlugin</th><td>Their service; the site says "Encrypted in transit and at rest"</td><td>Core $89 a year, Pro $180 a year, 7-day trial</td><td>Everyday users, many AI websites</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Hjarni</th><td>"EU-hosted (Hetzner)", "Encrypted in transit &amp; at rest"</td><td>Free up to 25 notes, Pro $12 a month</td><td>Everyday users and teams</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Basic Memory</th><td>Local: Markdown files on your computer. Cloud: their service</td><td>Local free, Cloud from $15 a month</td><td>People who like notes they can read and edit</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Supermemory</th><td>Their service by default; a free local version, set up with a terminal command, keeps it on your computer</td><td>Free with $5 of credits a month, Pro $19 a month</td><td>Mostly developers building with AI memory</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Mem0</th><td>Its MCP server: your Mem0 account, "not on your computer". Developers can also run Mem0 themselves</td><td>Free Hobby plan, Starter $19 a month</td><td>Developers building AI apps</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="learn-note">Prices and details are as of {CHECKED}, from each company's own website,
       and may change. Check the links below for the latest prices.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="apps">
    <h2>Each app in brief</h2>

    <h3>MemoryPlugin</h3>
    <p>MemoryPlugin works with a long list of AI apps, "21+ AI tools" in its own words, including
       ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Perplexity, Cursor and Claude Code. Your
       memories are kept by its service; its site says they are encrypted in transit and at rest
       and doesn't say where they're stored. Core is $89 a year after a 7-day trial
       (<a href={MEMORYPLUGIN}>MemoryPlugin's site</a>). Its reach into AI websites is wider than
       ours.</p>

    <h3>Hjarni</h3>
    <p>Hjarni is listed in the ChatGPT and Claude app directories and works with Cursor, Claude Code
       and other MCP apps. It says it is hosted in the EU on Hetzner, encrypted in transit and at
       rest. It has a free plan for up to 25 notes, then Pro is $12 a month, with two months free on
       yearly billing (<a href={HJARNI}>Hjarni's site</a>). If you want to try a shared memory for
       nothing, that free plan is a fair place to start.</p>

    <h3>Basic Memory</h3>
    <p>Basic Memory writes what your AI apps learn as plain Markdown notes, which you can open and
       edit yourself, in Obsidian for example. The local version is free and open source, and its
       site says "Your files never leave your computer"; you install it by following its
       quickstart. A Cloud version starts at $15 a month after a 7-day trial and needs no install
       (<a href={BASIC}>Basic Memory's site</a>, <a href={BASIC_DOCS}>its docs</a>). If you want
       to read your memory as notes, this is built for that.</p>

    <h3>Supermemory</h3>
    <p>Supermemory is a memory service with plugins for Claude Code and Cursor. You add its server,
       mcp.supermemory.ai, to your app and sign in, with no key to copy
       (<a href={SUPERMEMORY_MCP}>its setup page</a>). The free plan includes $5 of credits a
       month, and Pro is $19 a month (<a href={SUPERMEMORY}>Supermemory's pricing</a>). It is open
       source, and a free local version, started with a terminal command, keeps your memory in a
       folder on your computer (<a href={SUPERMEMORY_GITHUB}>its GitHub page</a>). Most of its
       plans are written for developers building with AI memory.</p>

    <h3>Mem0</h3>
    <p>Mem0 is memory for developers to build into their own AI apps, with a free Hobby plan and
       Starter at $19 a month (<a href={MEM0}>Mem0's pricing</a>). Its MCP server also connects
       Claude, Cursor and Codex directly, and its docs are clear that the memories "live in your
       Mem0 account, not on your computer" (<a href={MEM0_MCP}>Mem0's docs</a>). For a developer
       building an app, it is a strong choice.</p>

    <h3>Zaaheen</h3>
    <p>Zaaheen is ours: an app for Windows and Mac that keeps one memory for your AI apps,
       encrypted on your own computer, where our servers never receive it. It connects to Claude
       Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor, the ChatGPT app for your computer, Codex and other MCP apps,
       and installs like any other program.</p>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-compare"
      from="Cursor"
      to="ChatGPT"
      fact="Writes in British English"
      others={['Claude', 'Codex']}
      caption="With a memory kept on your computer, a preference you give Cursor is saved there once, and ChatGPT and your other connected apps read it from the same place."
    />
  </section>

  <section id="price">
    <h2>What a year costs</h2>
    <p>For one person on the first paid plan of each app made for everyday users, paying for a
       year:</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-year-cost"
      title="One year on the first paid plan, one person, in US dollars"
      measure="US dollars a year"
      bars={[
        { label: 'Zaaheen', value: PRICES.yearly, note: 'yearly plan' },
        { label: 'MemoryPlugin Core', value: 89, note: 'yearly plan' },
        { label: 'Hjarni Pro', value: 120, note: 'yearly billing' },
        { label: 'Basic Memory Cloud', value: 180, note: '12 months' },
      ]}
      source={{ text: `Each company's own website, prices as of ${CHECKED} and may change. Supermemory and Mem0 are left out because their plans are priced for developers. Hjarni and Basic Memory also have free options, described above.` }}
    />

    <p>Price isn't the whole story. A free plan that covers what you need beats any paid one, and
       Hjarni's free notes and Basic Memory's free local version are real options.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>Where Zaaheen falls short</h2>
    <p>A fair comparison has to say this part too:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>It runs on Windows and on Macs with Apple chips. There is no Linux or phone version.</li>
      <li>ChatGPT connects through the ChatGPT app for your computer. ChatGPT in a web browser or on
          your phone can't reach it. MemoryPlugin covers both, and Hjarni is listed in ChatGPT's own
          app directory.</li>
      <li>Your memory lives on one computer. There is no sync between computers yet.</li>
      <li>After the {TRIAL.days}-day trial there is no free plan.</li>
      <li>Some apps also need one line added to their instructions so they check the shared memory
          by themselves.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="choose">
    <h2>Which to choose</h2>
    <p>If you want your memories to stay on your own computer, and you use ChatGPT, Claude or
       Cursor as apps on Windows or a Mac, that is what we built Zaaheen for. If you mostly use AI
       in a web browser or on your phone, MemoryPlugin or Hjarni will reach more of the places you
       use it. If you want notes you can read and edit by hand, try Basic Memory. And if you're a
       developer building memory into your own product, look at Mem0 and Supermemory.</p>
    <p class="learn-note">MemoryPlugin, Hjarni, Basic Memory, Supermemory and Mem0 are names of
       their owners. We have no link with any of them. Facts checked on {CHECKED}; if anything here
       is wrong or out of date, tell us at <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.email}`}>{COMPANY.email}</a>
       and we'll correct it.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
